[2026-03-15T20:12:59.909Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JxiY7FjPY24XdwEXopp6AH","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Skill\n     2→\n     3→Bug reported by users for the app are tracked in `docs/bugReports.md`.\n     4→Open bug reports are listed at the start of this file, with later sections for reports that\n     5→were fixed and later reviewed.\n     6→\n     7→## Unpack Subtasks\n     8→\n     9→If there are any open bug reports in the file, add one task per bug using `add-task`.\n    10→Each bug gets the following subtasks in the task, performed in order. Unpack reports starting with\n    11→the oldest ones, which will be listed last.\n    12→\n    13→1. AnalyzeBug: Analyze and document the cause of the bug. Skip this for bugs asking for new functionality.\n    14→2. FixBug: Update the app to fix the bug\n    15→3. UpdateTests: Update the spec and tests to verify the fix and prevent regressions.\n    16→4. UpdateRevisions: Record any spec changes in AppRevisions.md.\n    17→5. ResolveBug: Mark the bug as resolved and in need of review.\n    18→\n    19→Example:\n    20→```bash\n    21→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    22→[{ \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/fixBugReport.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\n    23→  \"AnalyzeBug: Analyze <BugName>\",\n    24→  \"FixBug: Fix <BugName>\",\n    25→  \"UpdateTests: Update tests for <BugName>\",\n    26→  \"UpdateRevisions: Record spec changes for <BugName>\",\n    27→  \"ResolveBug: Mark <BugName> as resolved\"\n    28→]}]\n    29→EOF\n    30→```\n    31→\n    32→## Analyzing bugs\n    33→\n    34→If there is a recording ID referenced by the bug report, you MUST open it using Replay MCP\n    35→and explore it to understand what happened. Read this skill file to learn how to use these tools.\n    36→https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md\n    37→\n    38→Study the bug and identify the root cause of the problem by looking at the bug report, app source,\n    39→and any Replay MCP recording.\n    40→\n    41→Write a file docs/bugs/<BugReportName>.md with a complete explanation of what is causing the bug,\n    42→and update bugReports.md to refer to it.\n    43→\n    44→## Fixing bugs\n    45→\n    46→Read the guidelines and directives in `skills/tasks/build/writeApp.md` and then update the app's source to fix the bug.\n    47→\n    48→## Updating tests\n    49→\n    50→Review `docs/tests.md` to identify any tests relevant to the bug. Update these tests to reflect the\n    51→app's new behavior. If you did not find any relevant tests, add at least one new test entry which checks\n    52→that the bug doesn't occur.\n    53→\n    54→For any changes to the test spec you made, update the test files themselves to reflect those changes.\n    55→\n    56→After updating tests, make sure they pass. Read `skills/tasks/build/testing.md` to understand how to run tests and debug failures.\n    57→\n    58→## Updating revisions\n    59→\n    60→If the bug fix changed the app's behavior relative to the original `AppSpec.md` — for example,\n    61→adding new functionality, removing a feature, or changing how something works — update\n    62→`AppRevisions.md` (create the file next to `AppSpec.md` if it doesn't exist).\n    63→\n    64→`AppRevisions.md` is NOT a changelog. It is organized by topic sections that describe either\n    65→new functionality added to the app or changes made to the app spec in particular areas. Each\n    66→section has a heading describing the area (e.g., \"## Attachment Uploads\", \"## Authentication\",\n    67→\"## CSV Import\") and a description of the current state of that functionality.\n    68→\n    69→When updating revisions:\n    70→- If an existing section covers the area you changed, update that section's description to\n    71→  reflect the current behavior.\n    72→- If the change introduces a new area of functionality, add a new section.\n    73→- Keep descriptions focused on **what the app does now**, not a history of changes.\n    74→\n    75→If the fix was purely a code bug that didn't change the intended behavior, skip this step.\n    76→\n    77→## Resolving bugs\n    78→\n    79→Now that the bug is fixed, move it from the top section of the file to an \"Unreviewed\" section lower down.\n    80→Label the bug with the git revision before / after the bug was fixed and tests updated.\n    81→\n    82→Move the report to the appropriate section in `docs/bugReports.md`.\n    83→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a83e336-159e-4024-ba06-a40eb244cd7e","uuid":"271d16fc-4064-41c0-8913-0ca32ff7f260","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/tasks/maintain/fixBugReport.md","content":"# Skill\n\nBug reported by users for the app are tracked in `docs/bugReports.md`.\nOpen bug reports are listed at the start of this file, with later sections for reports that\nwere fixed and later reviewed.\n\n## Unpack Subtasks\n\nIf there are any open bug reports in the file, add one task per bug using `add-task`.\nEach bug gets the following subtasks in the task, performed in order. Unpack reports starting with\nthe oldest ones, which will be listed last.\n\n1. AnalyzeBug: Analyze and document the cause of the bug. Skip this for bugs asking for new functionality.\n2. FixBug: Update the app to fix the bug\n3. UpdateTests: Update the spec and tests to verify the fix and prevent regressions.\n4. UpdateRevisions: Record any spec changes in AppRevisions.md.\n5. ResolveBug: Mark the bug as resolved and in need of review.\n\nExample:\n```bash\nnpx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n[{ \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/fixBugReport.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\n  \"AnalyzeBug: Analyze <BugName>\",\n  \"FixBug: Fix <BugName>\",\n  \"UpdateTests: Update tests for <BugName>\",\n  \"UpdateRevisions: Record spec changes for <BugName>\",\n  \"ResolveBug: Mark <BugName> as resolved\"\n]}]\nEOF\n```\n\n## Analyzing bugs\n\nIf there is a recording ID referenced by the bug report, you MUST open it using Replay MCP\nand explore it to understand what happened. Read this skill file to learn how to use these tools.\nhttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md\n\nStudy the bug and identify the root cause of the problem by looking at the bug report, app source,\nand any Replay MCP recording.\n\nWrite a file docs/bugs/<BugReportName>.md with a complete explanation of what is causing the bug,\nand update bugReports.md to refer to it.\n\n## Fixing bugs\n\nRead the guidelines and directives in `skills/tasks/build/writeApp.md` and then update the app's source to fix the bug.\n\n## Updating tests\n\nReview `docs/tests.md` to identify any tests relevant to the bug. Update these tests to reflect the\napp's new behavior. If you did not find any relevant tests, add at least one new test entry which checks\nthat the bug doesn't occur.\n\nFor any changes to the test spec you made, update the test files themselves to reflect those changes.\n\nAfter updating tests, make sure they pass. Read `skills/tasks/build/testing.md` to understand how to run tests and debug failures.\n\n## Updating revisions\n\nIf the bug fix changed the app's behavior relative to the original `AppSpec.md` — for example,\nadding new functionality, removing a feature, or changing how something works — update\n`AppRevisions.md` (create the file next to `AppSpec.md` if it doesn't exist).\n\n`AppRevisions.md` is NOT a changelog. It is organized by topic sections that describe either\nnew functionality added to the app or changes made to the app spec in particular areas. Each\nsection has a heading describing the area (e.g., \"## Attachment Uploads\", \"## Authentication\",\n\"## CSV Import\") and a description of the current state of that functionality.\n\nWhen updating revisions:\n- If an existing section covers the area you changed, update that section's description to\n  reflect the current behavior.\n- If the change introduces a new area of functionality, add a new section.\n- Keep descriptions focused on **what the app does now**, not a history of changes.\n\nIf the fix was purely a code bug that didn't change the intended behavior, skip this step.\n\n## Resolving bugs\n\nNow that the bug is fixed, move it from the top section of the file to an \"Unreviewed\" section lower down.\nLabel the bug with the git revision before / after the bug was fixed and tests updated.\n\nMove the report to the appropriate section in `docs/bugReports.md`.\n","numLines":83,"startLine":1,"totalLines":83}}}
